<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n31598" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ob-ductĭo</span></b> ,
		ōnis, f.
		obduco, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a covering, veiling, enveloping</i>. *
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nubila inimica obductione pendent,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Arn. 1, 7</span></i>
			.— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			partic., <i>a veiling</i> of criminals before their
			execution: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">obductio capitis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Rab. Perd. 5, 16</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">capitum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Amm. 14, 7, 21</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Vulg. Eccles. 5, 1</span></i>; <i><span class="en">5,
				10</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
